==================================================
Lab 1: Basic Server-Side Template Injection (ERB)
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website shows a message on the home page through a URL parameter.
The server builds the page using an ERB template, and the message value is placed inside that template without being treated as plain text.
That lets us put template code in the message and have the server run it.

Goal: run the system command that deletes /home/carlos/morale.txt.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When the page loads with message=<text>, the server renders the text into the home page.
A normal message is shown as is, for example "Unfortunately this product is out of stock".

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The message value is inserted into the template before it is evaluated.
ERB reads anything between <%= and %> as Ruby code and prints the result, so the server runs what we send.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

ERB tag (<%= ... %>): Tells ERB to evaluate the expression inside and print its result.
Test expression: 7*7 is a simple check. If the page shows 49, the input is being evaluated as code.
system(): A Ruby method that runs an operating system command. It is the step that turns a test into an action.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

First, we send:

<%= 7*7 %>

The page shows 49 in place of the message, which confirms the template is evaluated.

Then we send:

<%= system("rm /home/carlos/morale.txt") %>

The server runs the rm command, which deletes Carlos's file. The page output does not matter here, only the effect on the server.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load the home page with message set to <%= 7*7 %>, URL-encoded.
Check that the page shows 49.
Replace the message with the system() payload, URL-encoded, and load the page. The lab is marked as solved once the file is deleted.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and sends a GET request to the home page with the message parameter.
* It sends the test payload first and checks for status 200 and the number 49 in the response.
* If the test passes, it sends the system() payload to delete the file.
* It sends the payload through params=, so requests encodes it correctly.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The application treats user input as part of the template rather than as data.
Any template expression in the input is evaluated by the server, including ones that run system commands.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Never build templates from user input. Pass the user value into the template as data, for example as a variable.
If user content must be shown, escape it so the template engine treats it as plain text.
Restrict what the web server process can do on the file system.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A template that evaluates user input is a code execution vulnerability.
A simple arithmetic test like 7*7 confirms the injection before a harmful payload is sent.
Never put user input into a template as code.
